INT. GENERATION SHIP OBSERVATION DOME - LATE SHIFT

A great curved window opens on a smear of unfamiliar stars. The dome is dim, the benches empty. A faint mechanical sigh from the air handlers. The far wall is a mural of an Earth long forgotten: green hills, a blue strip of ocean.

ELDER MIRIAM ACHTERBERG-OKOYE (70s, tall, hands knotted with age, a shawl the color of dried roses) sits on the center bench. Her granddaughter JUNIPER "JUNE" ACHTERBERG-ROSS (20s, engineer's overalls, a spanner in her belt) stands at the window. And between them, on the bench, SOLIS (a small cylinder with three glowing rings, the ship's companion unit) hovers.

JUNE
She says we're a decade out.

MIRIAM
I heard.

JUNE
She says the landing party leaves in a month.

MIRIAM
I heard that too.

SOLIS
(a chime, then a pleasant voice)
Elder Achterberg-Okoye. June. Your stress markers have elevated.

JUNE
Thanks, Solis. I noticed.

MIRIAM
The child is nervous, Solis.

SOLIS
She is permitted to be.

JUNE
I'm not a child.

MIRIAM
You're mine.

June turns from the window. The starlight cuts her face in half. She sits, hard, beside her grandmother, and Solis drifts a polite inch to the left.

JUNE
Gran. Did you know?

MIRIAM
Know what?

JUNE
That the planet isn't empty.

MIRIAM
(after a pause)
Who told you?

JUNE
I read the long-range logs. Last night.

MIRIAM
Those logs are sealed.

JUNE
I'm an engineer. Sealed things are my hobby.

SOLIS
June has violated Protocol Eleven.

JUNE
Solis, shut up.

SOLIS
(wounded chime)
I am only reporting.

MIRIAM
Solis, dim the dome.

The dome dims. The stars swell. Miriam folds her hands.

MIRIAM (CONT'D)
My mother knew. Her mother knew. It was in the founding charter, in a clause nobody read.

JUNE
What clause?

MIRIAM
"Upon arrival, the descendants shall be prepared to meet the descendants of those who stayed."

JUNE
Stayed where?

MIRIAM
On the new world. A second ship left Earth, twenty years before ours. Faster, bolder.

JUNE
Gran. They'd be--

MIRIAM
Dead. Yes. And their grandchildren would not.

June stands abruptly. Paces. The spanner clinks against her belt. Solis follows at a respectful distance.

JUNE
So we aren't the first.

MIRIAM
We never were.

JUNE
Then why does the council keep saying "pristine"?

MIRIAM
Because it's easier to land on a pristine world than on someone's home.

Silence. The stars crawl. Miriam reaches into her shawl and lifts out a small, worn photograph of a woman in a white coat standing by a hill.

MIRIAM (CONT'D)
That's her. My mother. She said: "If you ever learn the truth, you are to tell the next one."

JUNE
Me.

MIRIAM
You.

June takes the photograph. Holds it up to the dark window, where the hills in the picture seem to match, faintly, the shadows on the planet below.

SOLIS
Elder. June. Shall I log this conversation?

MIRIAM
Do not.

SOLIS
Understood.

JUNE
(quietly)
Solis. Delete the last ten minutes.

SOLIS
Deleted.

June sits again. Rests her head on Miriam's shoulder. Together, the three of them, one a machine, watch the new world roll slowly into view.
